---
/**
 * The monthly charge — the button that puts a shop on the site, and the one that stops paying for it.
 *
 * ── Why it is on the Payments tab and not in a wizard ──
 * It is the second half of the same errand as the two blocks above it: the bank account says where
 * the seller's money lands, the business details are what the processor examines, and this is what
 * he pays us. One screen owns money (owner, 2026-08-23).
 *
 * ── One charge, several shops ──
 * Since 2026-08-24 a plan is bought per shop and the seller has ONE standing order whose amount is
 * the sum of the shops he has on the site (`lib/store-plan.ts`). So this card has to answer two
 * different questions without mixing them: *what does THIS shop cost*, and *what is being charged to
 * my card in total*. The breakdown is rendered only when there is more than one line — with one
 * shop the total IS the plan, and printing it twice is how a screen starts looking complicated.
 *
 * ── The button leads OFF-SITE, and the copy says so ──
 * PayMe return their own payment page unless we hand them a card token, and we cannot: Hosted Fields
 * need the merchant's `seller_public_key` and **the merchant here is ours**, whose key was never
 * stored (`seller-subscription.ts`). Pretending otherwise — a spinner, then a redirect the seller
 * did not expect — is how a payment gets abandoned halfway. So the sentence under the button names
 * where it goes.
 *
 * ── Cancelling is a two-step, and the middle step is not a dark pattern ──
 * The owner asked for retention (*"כמובן צריך לנסות לשמר את הלקוח"*). What is offered is the two
 * things that are actually cheaper than leaving and that he may not know exist: a lower plan, and —
 * with more than one shop — taking one shop off the site instead of all of them. Both are real
 * alternatives to the thing he pressed. There is no "are you sure" loop and no offer he has to
 * decline twice: the cancel button is right there, in the same panel, at the same size.
 *
 * The sentence that does most of the work is the one about WHEN: he keeps everything to the end of
 * the month he already paid for (owner, 2026-08-24). A seller who knows that is a seller who is not
 * cancelling in a hurry to avoid being charged again.
 */
import { resolveTier, SELLER_SETUP_FEE, type SellerTierId } from '../../lib/pricing.js';
import { formatAgorot } from '../../lib/money.js';
import type { getT } from '../../i18n/index.js';

interface Props {
  /** The shop this card is about — every action it takes names it, because the plan and the line on
   *  the monthly charge belong to the shop rather than to the account. */
  storeId: string;
  /** This shop's plan. Absent falls back to the default, exactly as `resolveTier` does everywhere
   *  else — a bad stored value must never break this render. */
  tier: SellerTierId | undefined;
  /** What the plan is CALLED. It lives in the pricing copy rather than the dashboard's, so it is
   *  handed in rather than looked up here — this card would otherwise be the one component that
   *  reaches into another screen's translations to render a word.
   *
   *  Named at all because a seller who chose "צמיחה" met the word nowhere afterwards: the card said
   *  "125 ₪ לחודש" and left him to recognise his plan by its price (owner, 2026-08-24: *"מה קורה
   *  אחרי שעושים בחירת מסלול? איפה זה מופיע?"*). */
  planName: string;
  /** What is on file: `null` = none yet, `paying` = billing is running, otherwise it was created and
   *  nobody has paid it. `endsAt` is present only on a cancelled one still inside its paid period. */
  subscription: {
    status: number | null;
    nextCharge?: string;
    endsAt?: string;
    priceAgorot?: number;
    storeFees?: { storeId: string; storeName: string; tier: string; feeAgorot: number }[];
  } | null;
  paying: boolean;
  /** Whether THIS shop is one of the lines in the standing order. A paying seller who has just
   *  opened a second shop is `paying: true` and `storeInPlan: false` — the case where the card must
   *  offer to ADD a shop rather than to start a subscription. */
  storeInPlan?: boolean;
  /** A card is on file and nothing has been charged: he chose during PayMe's review and the first
   *  charge fires when the shop goes up (`lib/subscription-arm.ts`). */
  cardOnFile?: boolean;
  /** Who the card belongs to. PayMe's tokenizer wants a payer, and these are the seller's own
   *  account details — not a form for him to fill in again (`feedback_seller_form_burden`). */
  payer: { name: string; email: string };
  /** Rendered INSIDE a go-live step, which already carries the title and the one-line reason. Drops
   *  its own heading and its own frame, so the step is one thing rather than a box in a box saying
   *  "מנוי חודשי" twice (owner, 2026-08-24). */
  bare?: boolean;
  d: ReturnType<typeof getT>['dashboard'];
}
const { storeId, tier, planName, subscription, paying, storeInPlan = false, cardOnFile = false, payer, bare = false, d } = Astro.props;
const plan = resolveTier(tier);
const created = !!subscription;
/** A cancelled subscription still inside the period he paid for. Not the same as "not paying": he
 *  is, until this date, and the card must say so rather than offering him to start again. */
const endingOn = subscription?.endsAt;
const lines = subscription?.storeFees ?? [];
/** Only worth showing when the total is not simply this shop's plan. */
const showBreakdown = paying && lines.length > 1;
---

<section class={bare ? '' : 'card card--flat p-4 md:p-5 mt-4'} id="sub-card" data-paying={String(paying)} data-store={storeId}>
  {!bare && (
    <>
      <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0">{d.subTitle}</h3>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.84rem]">{d.subWhy}</p>
    </>
  )}

  {/* Label, colon, value, one per row — the shape facts take on this dashboard
      (`feedback_labelled_grid_over_prose`), rather than a sentence a seller has to parse to find
      his own commission rate in. */}
  <dl class={`grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-[0.86rem] m-0 ${bare ? '' : 'mt-4'}`}>
    {/* Dropped in `bare`, where the four plan pills sit directly above this grid and the marked one
        already carries the name and the price — repeating them a line later is the same fact twice
        in two shapes, which is what made the go-live step read as two cards. On the standalone card
        there are no pills, so the row is the only place the plan is named. */}
    {!bare && (
      <>
        <dt class="muted">{d.subPlan}</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 font-semibold">{d.subPlanValue.replace('{name}', planName).replace('{fee}', String(plan.monthlyFee)).replace('{per}', d.subPerMonth)}</dd>
      </>
    )}
    <dt class="muted">{d.subCommission}</dt>
    {/* Named so a plan change can repaint it without reloading the tab — it is the only figure on
        this card that the choice moves. */}
    <dd class="m-0 font-semibold" id="sub-commission">{plan.commissionPercent}%</dd>
    {showBreakdown && (
      <>
        {/* **The answer to "why am I charged ₪224".** One standing order, several shops — without
            this the seller reads a plan of ₪99 and a charge of ₪224 and has to guess at the
            arithmetic. Read from what the standing order was actually SET from, never re-derived,
            so this cannot disagree with his card statement. */}
        <dt class="muted">{d.subTotal}</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 font-semibold">{formatAgorot(subscription?.priceAgorot ?? 0)}</dd>
        <dt class="muted">{d.subTotalOf}</dt>
        <dd class="m-0">
          <ul role="list" class="list-none p-0 m-0 flex flex-col gap-0.5">
            {lines.map((line) => (
              <li class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                <span class={line.storeId === storeId ? 'font-semibold' : ''}>{line.storeName}</span>
                <span class="tabular-nums shrink-0">{formatAgorot(line.feeAgorot)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </dd>
      </>
    )}
    {paying && !endingOn && subscription?.nextCharge && (
      <>
        <dt class="muted">{d.subNextCharge}</dt>
        {/* PayMe's own string, passed through unparsed — it is theirs, and re-formatting it is how
            a displayed date and a billed date start disagreeing. */}
        <dd class="m-0 font-semibold" dir="ltr">{subscription.nextCharge}</dd>
      </>
    )}
  </dl>
  {/* Every number above is before VAT, and `pricing.ts`'s header argues at length why saying so is
      not optional: a seller who reads 99 and is invoiced 116.82 has been surprised by us on the one
      screen where trust is being established. */}
  <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.76rem]">{d.subVat}</p>

  <p class="hidden text-sm [color:var(--color-danger)] mt-3 mb-0" id="sub-error" role="alert"></p>

  {endingOn ? (
    /* Cancelled, and still his until the date. The one thing he must not be left guessing at, so it
       is the whole of what this state says — plus the way back, which is the same button that
       started it. */
    <div class="mt-4">
      <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subEnding.replace('{date}', endingOn.slice(0, 10))}</p>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.8rem]">{d.subEndingNote}</p>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm mt-3" id="sub-start" data-store={storeId}>{d.subResume}</button>
    </div>
  ) : paying && storeInPlan ? (
    <div class="mt-4">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subActive}</p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" id="sub-cancel">{d.subCancel}</button>
      </div>

      {/* ── The retention step ──
          Hidden until he presses cancel, and it is not a wall: the cancel button below is the same
          size as the alternatives beside it. What is offered is the two things that are genuinely
          cheaper than leaving and that he may not know are possible. */}
      <div class="!hidden mt-3 rounded-[var(--radius)] border [border-color:var(--color-border)] p-3" id="sub-cancel-panel">
        <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subCancelTitle}</p>
        {/* **With the DATE in it when we know one.** The rule alone — "until the end of the period
            you have paid for" — is a sentence a seller has to do arithmetic on at the one moment he
            is least inclined to. The date is PayMe's next-charge day, which is the same value shown
            a few rows above, so the two cannot disagree; without one (a subscription whose next
            date they have not returned) the sentence falls back to the rule, which is still true. */}
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.82rem]">
          {subscription?.nextCharge
            ? d.subCancelWhenDated.replace('{date}', subscription.nextCharge.slice(0, 10))
            : d.subCancelWhen}
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mt-3">
          <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" href="/pricing">{d.subCancelCheaper}</a>
          {lines.length > 1 && <span class="muted text-[0.8rem]">{d.subCancelOneStore}</span>}
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--sm" id="sub-cancel-confirm">{d.subCancelConfirm}</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : cardOnFile ? (
    /* ── Card on file, nothing charged ──
       The state a seller spends PayMe's whole review in since 2026-08-24. It has to say three
       things and no more: that we hold the card, what will be taken, and WHEN — because the one
       thing that turns a saved card into a complaint is a charge whose date nobody named. */
    <div class="mt-4">
      <p class="m-0 text-[0.86rem] font-semibold">{d.subCardOnFile}</p>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.8rem]">
        {d.subCardWhen.replace('{amount}', formatAgorot(subscription?.priceAgorot ?? 0))}
      </p>
      {/* A way to replace it, and deliberately quiet: an expired card between deciding and being
          approved is rare, and a prominent "change card" beside a saved one reads as doubt about
          whether it was saved. */}
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm mt-3" id="sub-card-replace">{d.subCardReplace}</button>
    </div>
  ) : paying && !storeInPlan ? (
    /* ── A shop being ADDED to a subscription that is already running ──
       He has a card PayMe are charging today, so there is nothing to type: one button, and the
       standing order grows by this shop's plan from the next charge. Falling through to the card
       fields would ask a paying seller to enter a card he has already given us, which is the one
       thing a second shop must not feel like (owner, 2026-08-25: *"אם מוכר פותח חנות חדשה וכבר יש
       לו חשבון סליקה, איך כל העניין הזה מופיע לו שם?"* — clearing is done, and so is the card). */
    <div class="mt-4">
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm" id="sub-start" data-store={storeId}>{d.subAddStore}</button>
      <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subAddStoreNote.replace('{fee}', String(plan.monthlyFee))}</p>
    </div>
  ) : (
    <div class="mt-4">
      {created && !paying && <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-[0.84rem]">{d.subAwaitingPayment}</p>}

      {/* ── PayMe's own card fields, drawn on OUR page ───────────────────────────────────────
          Three iframes served by PayMe and mounted here, so the seller commits at the moment he
          decides instead of a week later on somebody else's page. The container ids are the ones
          `scripts/checkout-card.ts` mounts into — the same three, because it is the same SDK call
          and duplicating them would be a second definition of a string that must match exactly.
          Hidden until the SDK answers: an empty bordered box where a card field should be is worse
          than no box at all, and `active: false` (no key configured) is a real state that falls
          back to PayMe's own page below. */}
      {/* What the tokenizer is told, carried as data rather than fetched again: the amount is the
          one this card already rendered, so the figure on the card issuer's own confirmation and
          the figure on this screen come from the same place. `firstName`/`lastName` is PayMe's
          split of a single Hebrew name — the first word and the rest, which is the honest reading
          of a name we hold as one string. */}
      <div
        class="!hidden mt-1"
        id="sub-card-fields"
        data-first-name={payer.name.trim().split(/\s+/)[0] ?? ''}
        data-last-name={payer.name.trim().split(/\s+/).slice(1).join(' ')}
        data-email={payer.email}
        data-label={d.subTitle}
        data-amount={(plan.monthlyFee).toFixed(2)}
      >
        {/* ── Fixed tracks, because these boxes contain IFRAMES ──
            An iframe has no intrinsic width to measure, so `auto` columns collapsed to nothing and
            `1fr` gave the rest away: the card number ended up a sliver with its label wrapped over
            two lines while תוקף and CVV took half the row each (owner's screenshot, 2026-08-25).
            Named tracks in `ch` instead — the card number keeps the row it needs, the two short
            fields take exactly what their content is, and nothing depends on measuring an empty
            frame. `minmax(0,…)` on the first so a long label cannot push the grid wider than its
            container.

            Each frame is given an explicit height too: an empty iframe is zero pixels tall until
            PayMe paint into it, which is what made the boxes different sizes from each other. */}
        <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,22ch)_9ch_7ch]">
          <div class="field m-0">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardNumber}</span>
            <div id="payme-card-number" class="input flex items-center h-[2.6rem] py-0"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="field m-0">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardExpiry}</span>
            <div id="payme-card-expiry" class="input flex items-center h-[2.6rem] py-0"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="field m-0">
            <span class="text-[0.8rem] whitespace-nowrap">{d.subCardCvc}</span>
            <div id="payme-card-cvc" class="input flex items-center h-[2.6rem] py-0"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm mt-3" id="sub-card-save" data-store={storeId}>{d.subCardSave}</button>
        {/* The sentence that makes the whole step acceptable, and it is the first thing under the
            button rather than the last thing on the card. */}
        <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-[0.8rem] font-semibold">{d.subCardNoCharge}</p>
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subSetupFee.replace('{amount}', String(SELLER_SETUP_FEE))}</p>
      </div>

      {/* A paying seller opening a SECOND shop is not starting a subscription — he is adding a line
          to one that is already running, and the button has to say that or it reads as a second
          monthly charge appearing from nowhere. */}
      {/* The fallback route, shown only when the card fields could not be drawn — a deployment
          whose own merchant was opened without keeping its public key (`payment-payme.ts`). The
          script un-hides whichever of the two is real, so a seller never sees both. */}
      {/* ── The fallback route, and everything about it lives INSIDE it ──
          These two sentences used to sit outside this box, so they printed under the card fields as
          well — a seller typing his card on our page was told he was being taken to the processor's
          page, and the setup-fee line appeared twice on one screen (owner, 2026-08-25: *"מה הכוונה
          מעבירים אותך לדף התשלום? והנה זה בדיוק כפילות שדיברתי עליה"*). They are true only of this
          route, so they belong to it. */}
      <div class="!hidden" id="sub-hosted-fallback">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--accent btn--sm" id="sub-start" data-store={storeId}>
          {created ? d.subResume : d.subStart}
        </button>
        {/* Said BEFORE the click, not after it: a redirect nobody was expecting is where a payment
            gets abandoned. */}
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-2 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subGoingToProcessor}</p>
        <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.78rem]">{d.subSetupFee.replace('{amount}', String(SELLER_SETUP_FEE))}</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  )}
</section>
